BEGIN;
DO $$
DECLARE
  additions jsonb := '{"course-1":"<section><h4>Start the lesson</h4><p>A financial plan connects the money a client has today with what they want to accomplish. A balance sheet is a snapshot of assets and debts. A cash-flow statement shows what came in and went out during a period. Net worth measures the snapshot. Cash surplus measures what could be redirected each month. A household can have a valuable home and still lack cash to pay next month’s bills.</p><h4>Follow the Rivera household</h4><p>Follow the Riveras. Take-home pay is $10,200 monthly. Essentials are $5,900, discretionary expenses $1,500 and debt service $1,300. Subtract all three outflows and you have $1,500 available. Their $22,000 cash covers about 3.7 months of the listed essentials. If their reserve target is six months of those essentials, it is $35,400. First confirm whether required debt payments should be included in the emergency budget. Before offering a product, ask how they rank reserves, the 19% credit-card balance and both children’s education.</p><h4>Practice before moving on</h4><p>Practice calculating reserves when the household treats $1,300 of debt service as essential. Find the six-month target and gap. Ask one neutral question to understand why the couple might disagree about how fast to pay the card down.</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>Six months × $7,200 = $43,200. Subtract $22,000 for a gap of $21,200. Try “What are you each most concerned would happen if we put more toward the card this month?” That surfaces whether the disagreement is about interest cost or access to cash.</p></details><p>After studying the topic lessons below, complete <a href=\"#case-1\">Applied case 1</a>. Explain your reasoning before opening the solution.</p></section>","course-2":"<section><h4>Start the lesson</h4><p>A risk can be avoided, reduced, retained or transferred. List each hazard, estimate its impact and decide what the household can absorb. A deductible is risk the client retains. Life insurance creates funds after death. Disability insurance usually replaces part of income while alive. Health insurance addresses covered medical expense. Check the actual benefit before counting it in a plan.</p><h4>Follow the Rivera household</h4><p>Alex has $100,000 of employer life. In a hypothetical survivor scenario, use $450,000 for the family’s income need in today’s dollars, $275,000 for mortgage payoff and $25,000 for transition costs. Existing group life and $50,000 of assets specifically available for these goals reduce the $750,000 need to $600,000. If Alex leaves the employer and coverage ends, the gap is $700,000. Check that the $50,000 is distinct from emergency reserves. Before advising on disability, request the benefit percentage, maximum, waiting period and occupational definition.</p><h4>Practice before moving on</h4><p>Assume the mortgage stays in place and the family’s $450,000 income estimate already includes its payments. What is the gap with and without employer life?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>Remove the separate $275,000 payoff to avoid double-counting. Total modeled need is $475,000. Subtract $50,000 of available assets and $100,000 of employer life for a $325,000 gap. Without employer life, the gap is $425,000.</p></details><p>After studying the topic lessons below, complete <a href=\"#case-2\">Applied case 2</a>. Explain your reasoning before opening the solution.</p></section>","course-3":"<section><h4>Start the lesson</h4><p>A stock is an ownership claim. A bond is a loan with contractual terms. A pooled fund holds underlying investments. A portfolio allocation describes how much of the household’s money is assigned to different assets. A target allocation starts with the purpose and the time the money can stay invested. Use diversification to reduce dependence on any one issuer while managing market exposure.</p><h4>Follow the Rivera household</h4><p>The Riveras need tuition for their older child in four years. Start by separating near-term education dollars from retirement investments with a much longer horizon. For an illustrative $500,000 retirement sleeve at a 60% stock target, begin with $300,000 in stocks. If market movements leave $340,000 of stocks and $160,000 of bonds, a $40,000 shift restores the target. An internal trade in a retirement account and a taxable sale can create different tax results. Check account-level positions and gains before selecting trades.</p><h4>Practice before moving on</h4><p>If the Riveras add $25,000 to bonds, what amount of stock now needs to be shifted to bonds to reach 60% stock?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>The portfolio is $525,000 and the 60% target is $315,000. Stocks remain $340,000 after the contribution, so shift $25,000 from stocks to bonds. Bonds then equal $210,000.</p></details><p>After studying the topic lessons below, complete <a href=\"#case-3\">Applied case 3</a>. Explain your reasoning before opening the solution.</p></section>","course-4":"<section><h4>Start the lesson</h4><p>Separate income, deductions, rates and credits. Taxable income is the amount on which ordinary bracket calculations generally start. The marginal rate is the rate on the next taxable dollar of a given type. The effective rate averages tax across a stated income base. For a decision, estimate incremental tax by comparing the return with and without the transaction.</p><h4>Follow the Rivera household</h4><p>The Riveras are comparing a hypothetical Roth conversion. Assume $86,000 of pre-conversion taxable income. In this practice example, incremental income through $100,000 is taxed at 12% and above it at 22%. A $24,000 conversion uses $14,000 at 12% and $10,000 at 22%, adding $3,880. This model isolates ordinary federal tax. Actual planning checks the exam-year tax table, account basis, credits, withholding and future rates. Run a second scenario using a smaller conversion and see whether paying tax today would consume reserves.</p><h4>Practice before moving on</h4><p>Under the same illustrative assumptions, compare a $10,000 conversion with a $20,000 conversion. What is each incremental tax and the effective incremental rate on the larger conversion?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>$10,000 × 12% = $1,200. On $20,000, tax is $14,000 × 12% + $6,000 × 22% = $3,000. Divide by $20,000 for 15%. The highest marginal rate applied in that scenario is 22%.</p></details><p>After studying the topic lessons below, complete <a href=\"#case-4\">Applied case 4</a>. Explain your reasoning before opening the solution.</p></section>","course-5":"<section><h4>Start the lesson</h4><p>The accumulation phase builds assets during work. The distribution phase uses assets to cover spending after accounting for reliable income. Separate pretax, Roth and taxable assets. Build a spending estimate, subtract dependable income, and divide the portfolio draw by investable assets. Keep all cash flows and return assumptions in the same dollars.</p><h4>Follow the Rivera household</h4><p>The Riveras want $85,000 of annual retirement spending and assume $48,000 of reliable income, all in today’s dollars. The portfolio gap is $37,000. At a hypothetical 4% initial withdrawal rate, a starting estimate is $925,000. At 3.5%, it is about $1.057 million. Neither calculation models a full retirement. Test lower returns, inflation, healthcare and the survivor’s income. Compare several options, such as saving more, retiring later or reducing spending.</p><h4>Practice before moving on</h4><p>At retirement, assume the portfolio is $925,000 and falls 20% before the first withdrawal. What draw rate supports the same spending? How much spending would you cut to restore a 4% initial draw, holding reliable income constant?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>Assets fall to $740,000. $37,000 ÷ $740,000 = 5%. A 4% draw would be $29,600, so annual spending would need to drop by $7,400 under these assumptions.</p></details><p>After studying the topic lessons below, complete <a href=\"#case-5\">Applied case 5</a>. Explain your reasoning before opening the solution.</p></section>","course-6":"<section><h4>Start the lesson</h4><p>Estate planning answers two questions about each asset. Who can manage it while the client is incapacitated, and who receives it after death? Start with legal owner, title, beneficiary form and controlling document. A will generally directs probate assets. A financial power of attorney can authorize someone to manage finances during life. A trust’s terms govern the assets held in it. Review documents with counsel.</p><h4>Follow the Rivera household</h4><p>The Riveras’ wills predate their second child. Gather current documents and beneficiary statements for every account. For each asset, trace who gets it, when it becomes accessible and whether that meets the goal. If a life policy names a child and the estate needs money, do not assume the proceeds are available for estate expenses. Counsel can help coordinate beneficiary designations, guardianship nominations and authority during incapacity.</p><h4>Practice before moving on</h4><p>The estate needs $80,000 quickly and has $45,000 of accessible cash. An adult child is named beneficiary on a $100,000 policy. What gap do you report and what would you verify?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>Report an initial $35,000 estate cash gap. Verify who receives the proceeds, any restrictions on access, and whether other funding can become available in time. A policy payment to the child is not part of estate-accessible cash by assumption.</p></details><p>After studying the topic lessons below, complete <a href=\"#case-6\">Applied case 6</a>. Explain your reasoning before opening the solution.</p></section>","capstone":"<section><h4>Start the lesson</h4><p>A useful plan puts the client’s goals in order and shows how the recommendations affect each other. Start with financially urgent needs and reversible steps. State assumptions. Compare the current path with alternatives, then give each approved step an owner, a deadline and evidence of completion.</p><h4>Follow the Rivera household</h4><p>For the Riveras, begin by confirming essential spending, the card balance and the employer match. Two paths might be to build reserves first or preserve a cash floor and put a portion of surplus toward the 19% card. Compare projected interest, time to build reserves and the ability to handle a surprise expense. Incorporate life and disability coverage, funds for both education goals, retirement sensitivities and an estate-document review. Explain why the same $1,500 of monthly surplus cannot be assigned to all goals at once.</p><h4>Practice before moving on</h4><p>Draft a three-step, 90-day plan. Allocate the current $1,500 monthly surplus once. State one event that would cause you to revise it. What would you say in the first minute of a client meeting?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>One defensible answer, after verifying essentials and match, directs $1,000 monthly toward the card and $500 toward reserves while keeping a verified cash floor. Assign benefit verification in the first 30 days and schedule a document review with counsel. At day 90, compare the actual card balance and reserve with projections, then revise if take-home pay falls. Open by explaining the trade-off between reducing high interest and keeping cash available for emergencies.</p></details><p>Next, use the capstone facts and scorecard below to develop and present your own plan.</p></section>","start":"<section><h4>New student learning path</h4><p>Start with Course 1. In each course, read the opening lesson, work the example, then try the practice exercise before revealing the solution. Read the related topic lessons and finish the applied case before moving on. The Riveras are a fictional household you’ll follow from the first meeting through the capstone.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#course-1\">Planning foundations</a></li><li><a href=\"#course-2\">Risk and employee benefits</a></li><li><a href=\"#course-3\">Investments and portfolio planning</a></li><li><a href=\"#course-4\">Federal tax planning</a></li><li><a href=\"#course-5\">Retirement savings and income</a></li><li><a href=\"#course-6\">Estate planning</a></li><li><a href=\"#capstone\">Financial plan development</a></li></ol></section>"}'::jsonb;
  existing jsonb;
BEGIN
  SELECT lessons INTO STRICT existing FROM public.tac_academy_courses
    WHERE slug = 'financial-planning-cfp-prep' FOR UPDATE;
  IF EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM jsonb_object_keys(additions) AS expected(id)
    WHERE (SELECT count(*) FROM jsonb_array_elements(existing) AS item WHERE item->>'id' = expected.id) <> 1
  ) THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'New student course targets do not match';
  END IF;
  UPDATE public.tac_academy_courses
    SET lessons = (
      SELECT jsonb_agg(
        CASE WHEN additions ? (item->>'id') AND strpos(item->>'html', additions->>(item->>'id')) = 0 THEN
          jsonb_set(item, '{html}', to_jsonb(
            left(item->>'html', strpos(item->>'html', '</h3>') + 4)
            || (additions->>(item->>'id'))
            || substr(item->>'html', strpos(item->>'html', '</h3>') + 5)
          ))
        ELSE item END ORDER BY ord
      )
      FROM jsonb_array_elements(existing) WITH ORDINALITY AS lesson(item, ord)
    )
    WHERE slug = 'financial-planning-cfp-prep';
END;
$$;
COMMIT;
